@meta
id: 22-04
title: Drill — the block and its braces
chapter: 22
type: drill
skills: visual-line
par: 16
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: click the fold arrow, select the folded line, Delete
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Eight times: delete the `if` block in `cancel` — the `if` line, its
body, and the closing brace. Three lines, gone, nothing else touched.

`%` jumps between matching brackets. Inside a line-wise selection it
takes every line between the two braces with it.

@keys
| `V` | line-wise selection |
| `$` | end of the line, where the `{` is |
| `%` | jump to the matching bracket, extending the selection |
| `d` | delete the selection |

@hints
Search for `if`, then `$` puts the cursor on the opening brace. `V`
before or after the `$`, it does not matter — line-wise does not care
about the column.
--
`/if` `Enter`, `$V%d`. Watch the three lines highlight before you press
`d`; that is the whole reason to do it this way rather than counting.

@solution
    /if<Enter> $ V % d :wq<Enter>

Twelve keystrokes. `d%` from the brace would delete character-wise
from `{` to `}` and leave the `if (...)` stub behind. Making it
line-wise first is what turns a bracket match into a block delete, and
it works on a three-line `if` and a three-hundred-line class alike.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });


    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}
